There are school trips you forget. And there are school trips that change everything. This one was the second kind.

The bus smelled like sunscreen, cheap energy drinks, and the nervous anticipation of thirty-two teenagers who would be on summer break in two weeks. Ember Blake sat in the third row, left side by the window, back straight, notebook balanced on her knees, because she had made a habit of looking, even during mandatory events, as if she had chosen to be there. Lane Tech slid away behind them, then Lake Shore Drive, then a billboard promising something about the future. Ember didn't like billboards about the future. They were too vague.

Behind her, three rows back, Trey Coleman laughed too loudly at something his wrist had said.

That was not a metaphor.

Trey wore a homemade watch with a homemade AI living on it. He had named it Bixi, and apparently it told jokes. Or at least, it told things Trey considered jokes. Ember had not yet decided which part was worse: that Trey talked to his watch, that the watch talked back, or that Trey would want to explain both to her the second she accidentally came within range.

She made a mental note not to come within range.

Henry Mills was sitting somewhere. Ember could not have said where. Henry had a talent for being as present in buses, classrooms, and group photos as an empty chair. She only noticed him once they got off, and even then only because he was holding on to his glasses while a wind that had no business existing in Chicago in the summer swept through the gap between the high-rises.

The Chicago Advanced Science Center was bigger than the photos on the school website had made it look. A glass facade that, in the morning sun, pretended there wasn't concrete hiding behind it. Above the entrance hung a logo that looked like the result of three workshops and a marketing agency. Inside, beyond a revolving door, was a lobby with a slowly rotating model of the galaxy, an oak reception desk, a visitor group in colored lanyards, and the usual smell of air-conditioning and floor cleaner.

"Please stay together," said the teacher, who had already given up on sounding strict. He held a class list, a laminated schedule, and the facial expression of a man with eight school days left to survive. "I'm going to split you into groups of three. These groups will stay together for the entire morning. The entire morning, Coleman."

"I didn't say anything," Trey said.

"You breathed."

Ember suppressed a smile, because she had made a habit of not rewarding teacher jokes. Instead, she opened her notebook, wrote the date in the upper corner, underlined it once, and thought about the two possible ways this morning could go: efficiently, or slowly.

The teacher read out names. Three. Three. Three.

"Blake, Coleman, Mills."

Ember heard her last name, and for a moment it was as if someone had turned down the volume in the lobby. She didn't look over. She didn't have to. She heard Trey, because Trey made a noise. It was not a word, more of an hm-okay, a nasal little sound that managed to be both amused and offended.

Henry said nothing. Henry rarely said anything when people might actually hear him.

"Meeting point by the pillar with your group number," the teacher said, gesturing vaguely toward three numbered pillars. "Tour starts in five."

Ember closed her notebook, inhaled once through her nose, exhaled once, and walked to the correct pillar. She had made a habit of knowing where she was going while she walked. It helped with a lot of things.

Trey approached from the left. Trey always approached from the left, at an angle, with too much momentum, as if walking in a straight line was a waste of time.

"Blake," he said instead of hello. "Ask me something."

"No."

"Ask me if Bixi detected three security cameras in the lobby today."

"No."

"Four."

"Trey."

"I'm just saying."

Henry appeared beside her without Ember noticing him arrive. He nodded at her, nodded at Trey, pushed his glasses up, and then looked down at the floor as if the floor were a discreet exit from every social situation. Ember registered that Henry was wearing a shirt that was too big for him, and that he had folded the collar inward twice, which did not help.

"Hi," Henry said quietly.

"Hi," Ember said in a tone that meant she knew who he was and was fine with that.

A woman in a navy-blue polo detached herself from the visitor group and came toward them, her lanyard swinging. She smiled the smile of someone who had given this talk four hundred times. "Lane Tech? You're my Lane Tech group? Great. We'll head out in a second, and I'll explain as we go. Phones on silent, no drinks in the labs, and if you have questions, please make them actual questions, not..." She looked at Trey, as if she had a sixth sense for Trey-types. "Not that."

"I hadn't said anything yet," Trey said.

"I know."

The woman led the group through an airlock of two glass doors, past a wall of sponsor logos. Ember recognized two of them, and one she didn't know caught her eye for a moment: Halverson Group. Small, white on dark gray, no slogan. She remembered logos without knowing why, roughly the way other people remembered song lyrics.

The hallway beyond was wider than it needed to be, with a linoleum-gray floor and a row of doors with magnetic locks. Above each door was a small light. Some were green. One was red. One flickered, yellow-green-yellow, as if it couldn't make up its mind.

"On the left," said the woman in the polo, "are our public areas. On the right is the research wing. We are not going in there, I promise, before anyone asks."

"I didn't ask," Trey said.

"You were about to."

Trey looked at Ember as if they had just shared an experience. Ember did not look back.

They kept walking. Henry stayed near the back of the group, Ember in the middle, Trey at the front, because Trey was always at the front whenever there was something to see. The woman explained something about materials research, the history of the building, an architect who had liked glass. Ember listened and didn't listen. There was a frequency on which she could store lectures without really experiencing them.

On the right side of the hallway, roughly level with the third door, something was wrong.

Ember didn't see it at first, and then she did. A door, heavier than the others, with no light above it and a gray sign at eye level. Dark background, white lettering.

AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY.

Trey slowed by half a step.

He kept going. But he looked back once more as they turned the corner, and Henry, who was behind them, saw him look back, and Ember saw that Henry saw Trey look back, and then they were in the next hallway, and the woman in the polo was saying something about the lobby and the rotating galaxy model and how they were about to start upstairs.

Ember opened her notebook again.

She didn't write anything.

Behind her, Trey whispered, "Bixi, just theoretically—"

"No," Ember said.

"You don't even know what I was going to ask."

"Yes, I do."

Bixi beeped once.

BIXI: PROBABLY YES.

Trey did not grin.

That was new.

Henry pushed his glasses up and tried very hard to look as if he were not part of this group.

The morning had begun according to plan. That was the problem with plans. They were usually the last to notice when they were over.

---

Trey stopped in front of the display case with the circuit boards because he wanted to see whether they were showing a real board or just some store-bought prop.

A prop.

Of course.

He turned on his heel, done with it, and at that exact moment his watch vibrated.

Not the normal pulse of a message. Three short bursts, one longer.

He raised his arm and tapped the display awake. Bixi's diagnostics layout slid into place, two thin curves overlapping, one of them twitchy.

"Electromagnetic anomaly," Trey read half-aloud from the display. "Unusual spectrum. Not consistent with visitor area."

He was mostly saying it to himself, but Henry, who had stayed two steps behind him by the display case, heard it anyway.

Henry heard everything without anyone noticing.

"What?" Henry asked quietly.

"Bixi's glitching. Or she isn't." Trey let his eyes travel along the wall, away from the display case, away from the main hall with the school groups, back toward where the light looked a little colder and the carpet ended and gave way to that scratchy industrial gray.

A door.

Half a shoulder-width from the end of the glass corridor. Smooth, painted, plain. On the narrow sign above the handle, in neat block letters, it said: AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY.

The kind of letters nobody printed for fun.

In front of it, about two steps from the threshold, lay a wrench.

Trey stopped.

The wrench wasn't some cartoon tool. It was new, a little oily at the jaw, with an orange tag on the handle that had a number on it. Behind it, a few yards farther in the maintenance area, he heard the muffled squeak of a rolling cart continuing on, unnoticed by whoever had dropped something.

The door should have fallen shut.

It hadn't quite latched.

A gap of maybe an inch.

Maybe an inch and a half.

Trey took a step closer. Bixi's curve twitched harder. He raised his wrist higher and angled it toward the gap. The curve spiked, once, briefly, then again.

"That's coming from in there," he said.

"Probably ventilation," Henry said. His voice was so small it sounded more like a theory offered to himself.

"Ventilation looks different."

"Trey."

Ember's voice came from behind him, not loud, but with that rail of steel in it she used on student council when someone was about to derail the agenda. She was half a hallway ahead of the main cluster of the class, already level with the display case, and stopped because she saw where he was looking.

"What are you doing?"

"Looking."

"Trey, that's a maintenance door."

"That's why I'm looking."

She came closer. Quickly, but in a way that didn't look like rushing. Ember moved through spaces like this as if she were always about to be in a photograph.

"It says AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY."

"I have eyes, Ember."

"Then act like you used them to read."

Trey lifted his wrist again and turned it so she could see the display. "Bixi picked up a signal that has no business being out here. That's all."

"What kind of signal?"

"I don't know."

"Great."

"She doesn't say what it is. She just says something doesn't fit."

Bixi delivered. Nothing more. The AI was not the all-knowing sidekick some of Trey's classmates probably imagined in their worse nightmares; she could tell that the spectrum didn't match the scenery, and that was where she left it.

Trey actually liked that about her.

Usually.

He bent down toward the wrench.

"Don't touch it," Ember said immediately.

"I'm just going to hang it on the handle. So someone notices it's missing."

"Don't pretend that's the idea."

He picked up the wrench anyway. Heavier than it looked. The oily jaw left a thin streak across his thumb. He stepped closer to the door, tilted his head sideways, and looked through the gap.

Farther back, in a corridor that had nothing to do with the visitor area anymore, a strip of cold light ran along the wall.

Not museum light.

Hospital light.

Farther down, the corridor turned.

"Trey."

"I'm just looking."

"Trey, step back."

From the main hall came the familiar clack of a tablet, a teacher's voice, something about "and then we'll gather at the model of the—" before the rest dissolved. Nobody was looking this way. This was a dead angle between the display case and the maintenance wall, where a wrench lay on the floor and a gap stood open and a watch quietly kept twitching.

Trey set his fingertips against the door.

"Coleman."

Ember used his last name because she knew it always cost one extra second of attention.

It cost one extra second of attention.

Then it didn't.

He pushed the door open another half inch. It moved reluctantly, because somewhere near the floor the seal dragged against the ground. Inside, it smelled like stale air-conditioning and a trace of machine oil, very faint, very orderly. No alarm. No sound except the low hum buildings like this always had.

"Just for a second," Trey said.

He said it to Ember.

He said it to Henry.

Mostly, he said it to himself.

He slipped through.

Trey would later call it curiosity.

Ember would call it something else.

The gap was smaller than he was. He got one shoulder through, then the rest of himself. His backpack brushed the doorframe. He heard Ember inhale sharply behind him, out in the visitor area, where everything was still fine.

"Trey," she whispered, and now it wasn't her student council voice anymore. Now it was quieter. "Come. Back. Out. Now."

He turned around in the corridor, holding the door with his foot. "Six feet. I'll look around the corner once. If there's nothing there, I'm back before they even miss us."

"And if there is something there?"

"Then six feet matters even more."

She looked at him through the gap. She looked behind herself, into the main hall, where the class was shifting in another direction. She looked at Henry.

Henry looked at the floor.

"Henry," she said.

"I know," Henry said.

"Tell him this is a bad idea."

"It's a bad idea."

"Thank you."

Henry didn't move. He stood with both hands on the strap of his backpack, as if he had to hold it in place so it wouldn't make a decision on its own. Behind his glasses, Trey could see Henry calculating.

Henry calculated a lot.

Sometimes he stopped calculating and simply did something.

Those were the moments Trey liked him best.

This was one of those moments.

Henry lifted his head, looked briefly at Ember, and then, narrow as he was, went through the gap without even taking off his backpack.

"Henry. Not you too."

"If he gets stuck in there, I don't want to explain from out here why he's stuck in there."

"Henry."

"Ember, just come with us."

She breathed out once, very controlled, the way people do in breathing-technique videos when the voiceover says, And now we let go.

Ember did not let go.

Ember never let go.

She looked over her shoulder one more time, checked the main hall, registered the teacher's tablet clacking, checked the angle of the cameras, half of which she probably couldn't even see, and then she did something that would run through her head several times over the next few days.

She followed.

She followed because she wanted to get him out.

She said that too, half-aloud as she climbed through, more to herself than to the others.

"I'm getting you out."

Trey nodded without turning around.

Behind them, the door did not click shut. The wrench lay where Trey had set it back down, half on the threshold, half on the gray flooring of the corridor where they had no business being. The door rested against the tool, open by an inch.

Exactly like before.

Bixi's curve kept twitching.

Trey swiped it away with his thumb and went around the corner.




---


The corridor was disappointing.

Trey had expected more from whatever was behind the maintenance door. Something with warning signs in a language he couldn't read. Instead: gray walls, gray linoleum, a row of narrow ceiling lights, every other one on. It didn't even smell interesting. It smelled like nothing.

"Congratulations," Ember whispered behind him. "You found a hallway."

"It keeps going."

"I don't know ... maybe we should go back," Henry mumbled.

Trey didn't hear him. He had already turned the watch on his wrist again, angling the little display inward, because he could read it better that way if he lifted his hand slightly. Bixi's indicator showed two bars. Outside the door, it had only shown one.

"Bixi says there's more RF in here."

"RF?" Henry whispered.

"Radio frequency," Trey said, like that made it better.

"Bixi is an app you wrote yourself," Ember said.

"Exactly."

For once, Ember had nothing for that.

Henry looked back toward the maintenance door, where the wrench was still jammed in the gap, the wrench no one had put there so three students could use it. From here, the gap was a thin line of light. Not far. But far enough that Henry calculated two steps before he said them out loud.

"Ember."

"I know."

"You're going farther too."

"So he doesn't break anything."

Henry followed.

Henry always followed.

The corridor bent left once, and beyond that it was shorter than Trey had hoped. A door, slightly open, a strip of light in the gap. He stopped in front of it because someone inside was talking.

Through the crack, the voices came in pieces. Enough to make you curious. Not enough to make you smart.

A woman's voice. Calm, measured.

"—by Friday. That is not negotiable, Scott."

"I'm not negotiating. I'm telling you it won't hold like this." A man's voice, lower, worn thin. "The readings from the second row were—"

"I saw the readings."

"Then you also saw what's happening in the third."

Trey made a gesture that could have meant anything. Henry understood it as don't breathe. Ember understood it as shut up, which was not exactly the same thing, but they were quiet.

They edged along the doorframe.

The room beyond was a maintenance room. Nothing more. Gray-painted steel shelves lined up along the left wall, tool cases, cable reels, a computer with two monitors on a workbench, a box of filter elements, a stack of safety goggles. It was neat. Too neat, Henry thought, though he would not have put it that way. A maintenance room visitors ever came near had a Coke can somewhere, a coffee cup, an old sticker on a locker. There was none of that here. There wasn't even dust on the top shelf of the rack he could see through the gap from where he stood.

He said nothing.

The two adults stood on the far side of the room, half hidden. Trey saw a lab coat and a white collar. A middle-aged woman, narrow, her hair tied back. A man, a little older, in a shirt with no tie, sleeves pushed up. One hand rested on the back of his neck, like someone who had not seen a chair in hours.

"I need Friday," she said. "The investors need Friday. The quarter—"

"The compound doesn't care about the quarter."

"Very poetic."

"Evelyn."

"Scott." She said his name as if she had already said it too many times. "I hear you. I heard you yesterday. The day before that. I'll hear you the day after tomorrow. And Friday will still be Friday."

Hartwell exhaled.

"If something gets through—"

"Then we carry that."

"We."

"Yes."

Trey leaned a little too far forward. Ember's hand settled on his back, at a point between his shoulder blades, and pushed him back. She did it without looking.

Something beeped on the workbench. Something beeped more softly on Trey's watch. He caught it, pressing his palm over it as if that solved anything. Bixi's display was up to three bars now.

Three.

In the visitor area upstairs, it had shown none in some places.

Voss said something he didn't quite catch because she had turned away. Something about the lower row and latest. Hartwell answered shortly. It did not sound like agreement.

"We can leave," Henry whispered. It was barely a voice, more breath than sound.

"In a second," Trey whispered.

"Now," Ember said, not whispering, but so quietly it was still a whisper.

"One second."

"Trey."

He didn't hear her, because Voss had sharpened in that moment, and he wanted the sentence.

"—it isn't stable. It never was. We knew that when we cleared the line. You're acting like this is a surprise."

"I'm not acting like anything. I'm telling you row three has been, since Monday—"

"I know."

"—and if someone—"

"I know, Scott."

A pause.

Trey saw Hartwell take his hand from his neck and look at her as if she had just told him too much.

Henry saw something else.

On the opposite wall, behind the shelves, there was a door. It wasn't painted like a maintenance-room door, but in the same matte color as the wall, so it almost disappeared. No handle. Just a narrow recess where someone could place a hand. Above the recess, at shoulder height, was a card reader with a small green diode. Above the reader, no sign. Nothing at all.

It was not a closet.

Closets didn't have card readers.

He said nothing.

The door made a sound he would not be able to describe later, because it was not a click and not a hum, but the kind of sound made by doors that were not built for visitors. It opened inward. The diode changed from green to white.

A man stepped out of the opening.

He wore blue coveralls, not a lab coat, with a stack of clipboards tucked under his left arm and a cup in his right hand. He did not look tired. He looked like someone for whom this door was part of the routine.

"Dr. Voss," he said, "the list—"

Trey moved first. He moved before he thought, and that was the best thing he had done all day, even if it didn't look like it. Ember moved at the same time, and Henry half a second later, which was enough. They were behind the second shelf before the man had finished speaking.

The shelf was tall enough.

Tall enough for three bodies pressed together, and for three breaths that were too loud and too fast and too close to the next person's head.

Trey was still covering his watch. It had stopped beeping. He had forgotten when he had turned on silent mode, or whether Bixi had done it herself, which was not actually possible, but he would think about that later.

Henry saw Ember's hand on the shelf. It was not steady. It lay flat, but her fingertips were white from pressing too hard. She was breathing through her mouth because it was quieter.

"—the list, yes," Voss said. "Not here. Come with me."

Footsteps.

Three pairs.

They went toward the first door, the one the three of them had come through, not the flush door in the wall the man had come out of.

The corridor.

Trey understood what that meant about half a second before Ember did.

They looked at each other.

The footsteps passed the gap in the door. They kept going. They grew softer. Farther down, another door—the maintenance door, the one with the wrench—was pulled open, and the sound a wrench made when it fell onto linoleum because someone opened a door was a sound Henry would remember for the rest of his life.

Silence.

"Shit," Trey said, very softly.

Ember said nothing.

Henry looked over at the flush door, closed again now, and at the diode, glowing green, and at the card reader where no hand rested anymore.

He said, "It goes farther down."

Ember turned her head.

"How do you know that?"

Henry shrugged, once, barely visibly.

"The cup in his hand was steaming."

Trey opened his mouth and closed it again. That was, barely, the smartest thing he had done all day.

Behind them, in the corridor, no one came back.



----

The freight elevator door started moving without anyone prompting it. A plain, mechanical sound. No warning tone, no flashing light, just the thin whir of the rail the steel panel slid along.

Trey turned his head first.

"Wait, wait, wait."

He said it to no one. Maybe to the door. He was standing in the corridor, phone angled in his hand, Bixi still open, and in the moment the door panels began moving toward each other, he took the step he would not be able to properly explain later.

Not in.

Not out.

Just one step too far.

"Trey." Ember's voice was sharp because it had to be sharp. "Get out of there."

She went after him. Three steps that did not feel like a decision, more like a correction. She had spent the entire morning pulling Trey out of rooms he had no business being in, and she saw no reason to stop now.

Henry stood still for one second.

One second in which he saw the empty, dim maintenance corridor, heard the faint hum of ventilation, and beyond it the elevator with the other two inside, and himself alone in a place where he was not supposed to be. He followed because that was the shorter option.

The door closed behind him, and in the second the lock clicked into place, he thought that he had really just stepped into a sealed elevator where no one had pressed a button.

"Okay," Trey said. "Okay, this is—"

The cab jolted.

Not hard. A brief drop, like a breath someone had taken too quickly. Then the elevator began to move, downward, steady, with no display showing which floor it was going to, no indication that anyone had called it at all. Above the door, a display glowed with green lines, and the lines counted down without numbers, without labels.

"Who pressed something?" Ember looked at Trey. "Trey. Did you press something?"

"No." He raised both hands, phone and all. "I swear. I swear, I—"

"Trey."

"I didn't touch anything, Ember Blake, I was by the door, I—"

She turned to the control panel. Two switches, a keyhole, one red button, all of it unlabeled or labeled in a code not meant for school trips. She pressed the red button. Nothing. She pressed it again, twice short, once long, as if at some point she had learned the proper way to press an emergency button, which she had not.

Henry stood with his back against the wall.

"I think it's getting faster," he said.

He was right. The whir had grown lower, the vibration in the floor had evened out into a constant, faintly unhealthy hum. They were going deeper than this building should have gone.

"Bixi," Trey said. It sounded like a reflex, not an idea. "Bixi, where are we?"

The phone in his hand showed the usual symbol, a thin circle that pulsed when Bixi responded. The circle did not pulse. Just gray standby, as if nobody were home. He tapped. Tapped again. Turned the screen off, then back on. The display lit up sluggishly, and in the top bar there was no signal. No bar, no symbol, nothing.

"Come on."

"Trey."

"One second."

"Trey."

He looked up. Ember's face had something in it he had not seen on her often. She did not look angry. She looked like someone who had just realized a list she had been keeping was wrong.

"My phone is dead," she said.

Not off.

Dead.

Henry pulled his from his pocket. Black screen. He pressed the button on the side. Nothing. He held it down longer. Nothing.

"Okay," he said quietly.

The green lines above the door went out one by one. Before Ember could turn that into a sentence, a sound came from above, muffled through concrete and steel, a sound that did not belong to the elevator. A long, clean startup, like ventilation wheels spooling up, and beneath it a second sound, deeper, something that felt more like a system than a machine.

The elevator stopped.

The door opened.

Not into a corridor.

Into an antechamber.

Low, bright, a dirty kind of bright. The light came from strips in the ceiling and fell onto a floor that was flawlessly clean. A few yards ahead was a second door, heavier, without a handle, with a glass pane behind which something moved that the reflection made impossible to see clearly. On the wall: an electrical cabinet, a yellow stripe, a small red light blinking at a steady rhythm.

No one stepped out.

"Ember." Henry's voice. "We should—"

"Yes."

She turned back to the elevator cab and pressed the buttons, all of them, in the order people press buttons when they are angry. The door stayed open. The elevator did not respond. Above the inside of the door, a symbol now glowed where there had not been one before: a simple lock, red.

"No," she said. "No, no, no."

Trey was already one step out of the cab. He looked around fast, with the look he got when something new was in front of him and he had not yet sorted it into place, a look that was sometimes gold and sometimes trouble.

"Trey, back in the elevator."

"It's locked," he said. "Look, the symbol—"

"Trey."

Behind the glass pane of the second door, the light grew brighter. A different kind of bright. Not warm, not white, more like the light a camera makes right before you feel the flash. A voice, far away. Several voices, through two walls. One said something that sounded like a number. Another said something no one understood because it was not meant for them.

Henry said, "What is that?"

No one answered, because no one knew.

The little light on the wall changed from red to green.

It happened fast.

Faster than a body could react to. The glass pane in the second door turned opaque for a moment, white, and then not white anymore, but something that did not belong to any color, a brief blooming, a pressure that did not come through the ears but through the chest. Ember felt her own breath stop, not from fear, but because something was holding it still. Trey said something, or started to, and the sentence broke off in the middle as if someone had taken the word out of his mouth. Henry dropped toward his knees, not all the way, halfway, a step that never finished.

It smelled like nothing.

That was the strangest part. It smelled like nothing, and at the same time it smelled like metal, ozone, something sweet that was not sweet, and the inside of a mouth after biting foil. The strips of light in the ceiling flickered once, not frantically, more like someone blinking. The hum that had come from above before was now coming from the floor, from the walls, from inside their own chests.

It lasted maybe two seconds.

Maybe three.

Then it was gone.

The little light was red again. The ceiling lights glowed normally. Behind the glass pane, nothing concrete could be seen anymore, only movement, different now, faster, more coordinated. Someone said something that sounded like an instruction, and no one looked their way.

Ember was still standing.

She stood with one hand against the elevator wall, and she realized she was holding on to it, and she realized she was not doing it because she had lost her balance, but because her knees had not decided whether they were going to hold her up. She pulled her hand away. Then she put it back against the wall.

Henry had made it all the way down, half sitting, half crouching, his glasses slightly crooked, and he looked at Ember, then Trey, then Ember again. He opened his mouth and closed it.

Trey stood where he had been standing. He was still holding the phone, and the display was on. The signal bars were back. Bixi's circle was pulsing, slow, normal, as if nothing had happened.

"Did you guys—" Trey began.

He did not get any further.

Behind Ember, inside the elevator, something started moving. The red lock symbol went out. The doors began to close from the cab side, slowly, with the same calm whir as before, and Ember reached between them without thinking. The door held. A sensor gave way. She pulled Henry up by his jacket. Trey was already half inside. Then all three of them were in the cab, and the elevator moved.

Up.

No one pressed a button.

The elevator moved, and they stood inside it, three teenagers: one with crooked glasses, one with her hand still against the wall, one with a phone whose battery now showed twenty-one percent even though it had been at eighty-seven ten minutes ago. No one said anything. The green lines above the door counted back.

The door opened.

Maintenance corridor.

Dim.

Ventilation.

Empty.

They stepped out.

Behind them, the elevator closed. The whir disappeared into the depth below. In the hallway, their own footsteps lingered longer than footsteps normally did.

"What," Trey said, "was that?"

No one answered.

Henry adjusted his glasses. Ember looked at the hand she had had against the wall, then at the floor, then down the corridor they had come through, checking which way led back. Trey looked at his phone, at Bixi, at the display, which showed nothing unusual.

They walked.

Behind them, far below, in an antechamber where no one was standing anymore, a little light blinked green once, red once, and then settled on red.



----


The corridor was shorter than Henry remembered it.

Earlier, every yard had been its own decision. Now the distance simply disappeared behind them, as if someone were rolling up a stretched-out tape. Trey walked in front, Ember in the middle, Henry last. That was not how they had started.

That was how they went back.

The maintenance door was still open exactly the way it had been before. The wrench lay beneath the bottom edge of the door, light gray, dirty, a tool someone would miss as soon as they started looking for it. Trey did not pick it up. Henry saw Trey look at it for half a second too long, and then keep walking.

It was the first time Henry had ever seen Trey not touch something that was just lying out in the open.

Beyond the door was the public area. Henry heard it before he saw it: the echo of a school group in a tall room, the squeak of sneakers on linoleum, a teacher's voice calling a name without really calling. Sounds he knew. Sounds he usually disappeared into like background noise.

Ember stopped one step before the door. She lifted a hand to the hem of her jacket and smoothed it down. Then did it again. Henry saw her fingers.

They were not completely steady.

"You go first," she said. Her voice was normal. Henry heard where she had put the normal on anyway.

Trey slipped past her. "I'll check if anyone's looking."

"Trey."

"I'm just checking."

He was already outside. Henry heard the sound change: the school group moved closer without moving. Ember exhaled once, controlled, through her nose. Then she nodded to Henry, as if he were the one who needed instructions now.

He went.

The transition was absurdly unremarkable. One door, one step, a different floor, different light. No sirens. No voices shouting anything. Just the exhibit they had been in before, and at the far wall the class gathering around a display case with something glowing inside it that Henry could no longer quite identify. Someone laughed. Someone said something about lunch. A teacher counted under her breath.

Henry's glasses were crooked. He only noticed when he automatically pushed them straight, and then he noticed that his fingers had to touch the frames twice before they sat where they were supposed to. He lowered his hand. He decided not to raise it again right away.

Trey had leaned against a pillar as if he had been standing there the whole time. He was looking at Bixi. There was nothing on the display Henry could make out. Trey looked anyway. He swiped once, then again, then held the device for a moment as if he had to wait for it to answer him.

Ember came out last. She closed the door behind her, not hurriedly, with her shoulder, almost casually. She did not look back. Henry saw her hand stay on the doorframe for a second.

And then not anymore.

"There you are."

A girl from their class, one Henry knew by sight, whose name he could never reliably remember. She said it without accusation. It was not a question. It was as casual as only sentences can be when they accidentally land on something.

"Bathroom," Ember said. She said it so lightly that Henry briefly thought she was right. "And then I lost these two."

"Classic," the girl said, already turning away.

Henry only realized afterward that he had been holding his breath. He let it out slowly, because he was afraid it would be noticeable if he did it quickly.

No one had looked.

Trey came up beside him, too close, a little too fast. "You good?"

It was not a question.

"Yeah," Henry said.

"You sure."

"Yeah."

Trey looked at him for half a second. A full one. Henry knew that look. Trey made that look when he wanted to know something and did not want to ask. Henry knew the look from the other side too.

"Okay," Trey said.

He said it to himself.

The teacher finished counting. She landed on a number that apparently worked, because she nodded and waved the class onward, toward the hallway that led to the atrium. Henry went with them. He walked in the middle of the group, where he usually walked. He walked there because people did not count him there; they estimated around him.

Today he was grateful for that, and that made him uneasy.

Ember had gone up front, to the teacher. She said something that made the teacher laugh. Henry watched her back and thought he would never understand how she did that. A few minutes ago, her hand had not been steady. Now a teacher was laughing.

Trey stayed beside him. He put the phone into his inside pocket. Two steps later, he took it back out. Then he put it away again.

Henry saw it and said nothing.

"So," Trey began quietly, and Henry knew before the comma that Trey did not want to keep talking. Trey knew it himself. He did not finish the sentence.

It was the most astonishing thing Henry had seen from him in months.

They kept walking.

The atrium was brighter. Glass, steel, the usual weight of architecture, which Henry normally liked because it was orderly. A group of elementary school kids stood in front of a model he did not recognize. A woman in uniform stood by a door that was not her door and looked down at her radio without speaking. Henry waited for her to look up.

She did not look up.

The teacher gathered the class in front of the coat area. Jackets, backpacks, the usual mess. Ember helped another girl with a zipper. Trey left Bixi in his pocket. Henry put his jacket on one arm at a time, and when he brought the second arm through, his glasses were crooked again. He reached up. Stopped himself. Reached up anyway.

No one talked about the door. No one talked about the wrench. No one talked about the bright moment when all three of them, for one instant, had stopped knowing what would happen next. It was not even an agreement. It was as if each of them had chosen not to start the same sentence.

The teacher counted again, out of habit.

She reached the same number.

Henry looked at Ember. She had her hands in her jacket pockets and stood in a posture he knew from the school hallway: friendly, straight-backed, present. He looked at Trey. Trey was leaning against the railing, one hand on the display, one leg bent, watching a group of adults pass through the revolving door.

It looked like before.

It looked exactly like before, and that was what felt wrong.

Henry could not have explained what had changed. The lobby was the same. The voices were the same. The light on the floor was the same.

And still, it felt as if someone had moved something half an inch out of place.

The woman with the radio still had not spoken.

Henry realized he had been watching her too long.

He looked away.

The bus was waiting outside the revolving door. Henry held his glasses in place with two fingers as he stepped outside, and only realized once he was out there that he had not needed to.

