Wednesday, third period, break hallway.

Ember stood with her back to the row of lockers, a stack of folders pressed against her stomach, trying to explain to a girl from the other class why she could not simply move the reservation for the meeting room on Friday. It was not, in itself, a crisis. It was one of those small conversations Ember had three times a week and could have in her sleep.

The girl was talking faster than Ember liked. Somewhere between "But I need the room" and "You always do this," something sank lower in Ember's chest, and she did not even raise her voice very much. She only said:

"Stop."

It was not a loud word. It was quiet, if anything. But it hit the hallway like a book being slammed shut.

The girl went silent in the middle of her sentence. Her chin dipped half an inch, her eyes got too wide, and then she said, almost tonelessly, "Sorry, you're right, I—sorry."

She stood there for another two seconds, as if she had forgotten something, then turned and left. Three girls who had been standing by the lockers across the hall stopped typing and looked at Ember. One of them smiled uncertainly, as if waiting for a punch line.

Ember smiled back, because that was the kind of response she always had ready. Inside, she started calculating. She had not raised her voice. She had not even gestured. She had said two words.

She slipped past the girls into the bathroom and set the folders down on the sink. The light above the mirror hummed. She turned on the faucet because she needed something to do with her hands, and wondered what, exactly, had just happened between her voice and the hallway.

The moment she turned the water off again, she thought, concretely, for the first time in days, about the maintenance corridor at the Chicago Advanced Science Center. About the open door. About the smell that had been different for one second.

Henry stood two hours later at the end of the main hallway, half behind the pillar with the fire alarm set into it. He had not consciously chosen to stand there. He simply always stood there, because from that spot, the way to the biology wing was easiest to see.

Zoe came out of the stairwell. She was the most popular girl in school, which was not Henry's opinion so much as a statistical fact everyone knew. Henry had maybe exchanged four sentences with her since eighth grade, and three of them had been "Sorry."

She came straight toward him.

Not to him.

Along the line he was standing on.

Henry stepped half a step aside, because he always did, but this time half a step was not enough, and he stopped for a moment because he thought she would move around him.

She did not.

She just kept walking, past his sleeve, close enough that he felt the fabric of her backpack strap brush the back of his hand. She did not look at him. She did not look away from him. Her gaze rested on a point behind him, somewhere near the poster for the sports festival, and her face wore the calm, polite expression people had when they were walking down an empty hallway.

Henry stayed where he was. His heart was beating too fast, which he was used to. What he was not used to was not knowing what had just happened.

She had seen him. She had to have seen him. He had been standing by the pillar. She had walked past him with eight inches to spare.

Or she had not seen him.

He turned his head and watched her go. She stopped at the next notice board, talked to another girl, laughed. Her eyes passed briefly in his direction without landing.

Henry tightened the strap of his backpack. He tried not to think about it for the rest of the afternoon, and thought about it for the rest of the afternoon.

Trey got the message from his father by text, which was already a bad sign. His father did not text when things were fine. His father called when things were fine.

Have to return the tickets. We'll talk tonight.

That was all. Trey read it on the bus, read it again at the stop, read it a third time in front of his house, and put his phone away before he rang the bell because he did not want to walk in with the screen in his hand.

The conversation did not take long. His father explained the financial part calmly, which was worse than yelling. The air conditioner in the kitchen needed to be repaired. The tickets were an expense that had to go. The concert night with Diego and Marcus, the one Trey had been saving for for three months, the one his father had covered the last two tickets for, was canceled, very calmly, very finally, and at the end Trey only said "Understood" and went upstairs to his room.

He closed the door. He did not slam it. He stood in the middle of the room for two seconds and breathed out once.

The desk lamp flickered.

It was brief, a twitch, like when someone in the stairwell hit a light switch too fast. Trey looked over, not because it was unusual, but because his eyes had to go somewhere.

His phone on the bed vibrated once, briefly, with no notification.

Then again.

On the desk, beside the monitor, the little display where Bixi normally showed a calm status line began to blink. It flickered. Not off, not on. Something in between.

"Bixi," Trey said. His voice was rougher than he had expected.

"Here," Bixi answered. The voice caught once in the middle of the word. "Voltage... fluctuation. Local. Source un—"

The display froze.

Then it continued, normal.

"Huh," Trey said, very quietly.

He went to the desk. The lamp had settled. The phone lay still. Bixi was showing her status line again, as if nothing had happened.

He did not touch the lamp. He sat down on the edge of the bed and stared at the desk and thought about the maintenance corridor.

Thursday, lunch break.

Ember stood in the main hallway because her locker was in the main hallway. She saw Henry by the pillar, where Henry Mills always stood, and she saw Trey, who was unusually quiet that day as he moved through the hall and was not talking to Diego or Marcus.

It was not a decision.

It was more like every other possibility falling away.

She went to Henry. Henry saw her coming and adjusted the strap of his backpack, which was his pillar reflex.

"Hi," Ember said.

"Hi," Henry said.

Ember hesitated. Ember did not hesitate often. "You were in that hallway during the field trip," she said.

Henry looked at her. Something in his face adjusted itself.

"Yes," he said.

Ember nodded once, briefly. "Do you have fifth period free today?"

"Sixth," Henry said.

"Sixth works too," Ember said.

They stood there for a second. Then Trey slid up to the lockers from the other side and pretended to look for something. He did not look at Ember or Henry. He said to his locker, "Weird crap happening to you guys too?"

Ember looked at Henry.

Henry looked at Ember.

"Yes," Ember said.

"Yes," Henry said, quieter.

Trey closed his locker without having taken anything out.

"Library," he said. "Back where the old atlases are. Nobody goes there."

"Last period," Ember said.

"Before break," Trey added.

"That was implied," Ember said.

Henry nodded. He nodded twice, because the first nod had been too small.

They stood there for another moment, three people who officially had nothing to do with one another, at the same block of lockers, with no smiles, no small talk. Then Trey went left, Henry went right, and Ember stayed by her locker until the hallway had emptied.

She realized she was holding the folder in her hand too tightly. She loosened her grip. She exhaled once, controlled, through her nose, the way she had been taught.

In the last period before summer break, back by the atlases, she would meet two people she would not have had anything to discuss with before Monday.

That was new.

But that was not the new part.

The new part was that she was relieved.



